A correction container is a thin-walled, cup- or sleeve-shaped insert whose outer surface conforms to the central well of a Marinelli beaker and whose inner cavity fits over the detector head. It may include a locating flange or shoulder to maintain a fixed axial and radial position and a sealed annular space containing a known reference or correction material.

Placed between the sample beaker and detector, it:

- provides a reproducible measurement geometry;
- enables efficiency, attenuation, and self-absorption corrections under nearly the same conditions as the actual sample;
- reduces errors caused by differences in sample density, fill level, or beaker positioning;
- avoids direct contact with and contamination of the detector;
- can be installed or exchanged without modifying the detector or Marinelli beaker; and
- improves nuclide-identification and activity-quantification accuracy while simplifying calibration.
